<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">British Honduras</h1><p class="st-meta">1862 CE – 1981 CE · capital Belize City; later Belmopan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Britain's only colony on the Central American mainland, a mahogany and chicle territory claimed by Guatemala that became independent Belize in 1981.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1862 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> formally declared the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/belize-settlement">Belize Settlement</a> a colony, under the name British <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Honduras</a>, subordinate to the governor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. Treaties with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Guatemala</a> in 1859 and with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1893 fixed its borders, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> later claimed the whole territory, arguing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had not kept a promise to build a road.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The settlers' assembly gave up its powers in 1871, making British <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Honduras</a> a crown colony, and in 1884 it received its own governor. Mahogany, logwood and later chicle for chewing gum were the main exports, controlled by a few British firms, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize-estate-and-produce-company">Belize Estate and Produce Company</a>. The population mixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creoles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garifuna">Garifuna</a>, Maya and Mestizo refugees from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste-war-of-yucatan">Caste War</a>. A hurricane devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize-city">Belize City</a> in 1931.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Currency devaluation in 1949 sparked the nationalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-united-party">People's United Party</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-cadle-price">George Price</a>. Self-government came in 1964, and the colony was renamed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/belize">Belize</a> in 1973. After Hurricane Hattie, the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belmopan">Belmopan</a> in 1970, and Belize became independent on 21 September 1981, with British troops guarding it against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Guatemala</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-honduras.jpg" alt="British Honduras" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Heraldry · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_British_Honduras_(1919%E2%80%931981).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1862 CE – 1981 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Belize City; later Belmopan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>George Price</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism and Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English and Belizean Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belize">Belize</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony of British Honduras</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1922&amp;focus=british-honduras" aria-label="British Honduras on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2268 1544 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where British Honduras lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2588 1800L2605 1784M2588 1800L4997 651M2588 1800L2587 1793"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2605 1784h0M4997 651h0M2587 1793h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2588 1800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2588 1800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1922&amp;focus=british-honduras">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
